{"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Omega", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " (uppercase ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "Ω", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ", lowercase ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "ω", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ") is the twenty-fourth and last letter of the Greek alphabet. Because it closes the alphabet, omega has come to mean \"the end\" or \"the last,\" most famously in the phrase ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "alpha and omega", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 2, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ". In science the two cases have separate, well-established jobs: Ω is the symbol for the ohm, the unit of electrical resistance, and ω stands for angular velocity and angular frequency in physics.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h2", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Common Omega Expressions", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"tag": "ul", "type": "list", "start": 1, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "listitem", "value": 1, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "R = 100 Ω", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " — a resistance value", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 2, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Z = 50 Ω", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " — characteristic impedance", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 3, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "ω = 2πf", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " — angular frequency", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 4, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "ω = v/r", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " — angular velocity", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 5, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Ω(n)", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " — asymptotic lower bound", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 6, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Ω = 4π sr", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " — solid angle of a full sphere", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}], "listType": "bullet", "direction": null}, {"tag": "h2", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Frequently Asked Questions", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"tag": "h3", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What is the definition of omega?", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Omega is the last letter of the Greek alphabet, written Ω in uppercase and ω in lowercase. Figuratively it means \"the end\" or \"the last,\" and in science it is the symbol for the ohm (Ω) and for angular velocity or angular frequency (ω).", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h3", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What does omega mean in math?", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "In mathematics, uppercase Ω is Big Omega, an asymptotic lower bound (f(n) = Ω(n log n)), the sample space in probability, a solid angle, or a region of integration. Lowercase ω is the first infinite ordinal in set theory, a primitive cube root of unity (ω³ = 1), and little omega in complexity theory.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h3", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What does Ω mean in electricity?", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Ω is the ohm, the SI unit of electrical resistance. A component marked 220 Ω resists current such that 220 volts would drive one ampere through it.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h3", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What does ω mean in physics?", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Lowercase ω is angular velocity (radians per second) in rotational motion, or angular frequency ω = 2πf in waves and oscillations.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h3", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Is the ohm symbol the same as the Greek letter omega?", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Visually yes, and in practice yes. Unicode has a separate ohm sign (U+2126) for legacy compatibility, but it recommends using the Greek letter (U+03A9), and normalization converts one into the other.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h3", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What does \"alpha and omega\" mean?", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "It means the beginning and the end, or the whole of something. The phrase comes from the Book of Revelation, where it is a title for God.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h3", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What is the difference between uppercase Ω and lowercase ω?", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "They are the same letter in two cases with different jobs. Uppercase Ω (U+03A9) is the ohm sign, Big Omega and the sample space; lowercase ω (U+03C9) is angular velocity, angular frequency and the first infinite ordinal. In LaTeX the case of the command decides which you get: ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "\\Omega", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 16, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " or ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "\\omega", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 16, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ".", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h3", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "How do you type the omega symbol?", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "On Windows, hold Alt and type 234 on the numeric keypad for Ω; for ω type 03C9 and press Alt + X in Word. On a Mac, Option + Z gives Ω, and the Greek input source gives ω. In HTML use &Omega; and &omega;, or simply copy Ω and ω from the box at the top of this page.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h3", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "How do you write omega in LaTeX?", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Use ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "$\\Omega$", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 16, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " for uppercase and ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "$\\omega$", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 16, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " for lowercase, always in math mode. For the ohm unit in text, use ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "\\qty{100}{\\ohm}", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 16, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " from siunitx or ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "\\textohm", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 16, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "; for a bold omega use ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "\\bm{\\omega}", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 16, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ".", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h2", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Writing Technical Documents", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Circuit descriptions, mechanics problems, and cosmology papers all depend on omega being typeset correctly, and a document that mixes Ω with \"ohm,\" or ω with \"w,\" reads as careless. In LaTeX, ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "\\Omega", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 16, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " and ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "\\omega", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 16, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " in math mode, with siunitx for units, keep every instance consistent.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Octree is an AI-powered LaTeX editor that handles Greek letters, units, and equations for you, so you can focus on the engineering instead of the markup.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Create professional documents at https://useoctree.com", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}], "direction": null}}
